---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'Solar Panel Cleaning Services Thailand | PV Maintenance | JJ Com Solar Cell';
const description = 'Professional solar panel cleaning and maintenance in Koh Chang, Thailand. Remove dust, salt, bird droppings and debris from photovoltaic modules. Restore system efficiency. 300 THB per panel.';
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="website"
  jsonLd={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Service",
    "name": "Solar Panel Cleaning and Maintenance",
    "provider": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "JJ Com Solar Cell" },
    "areaServed": { "@type": "Country", "name": "Thailand" }
  }}
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-solar-green">Home</a> / <span>Services</span> / <span>Panel Cleaning</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="text-center mb-12">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-solar-yellow/20 text-solar-yellow px-4 py-2 rounded-full text-sm font-medium mb-6">
        Serving Thailand Since 2016
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">Solar Panel Cleaning and Maintenance</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Restore lost energy production from soiled photovoltaic modules. Professional cleaning, inspection, and preventive maintenance across Thailand.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-12">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-solar-green mb-2">300</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-gray-600">THB per panel cleaned</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-solar-green mb-2">15-25%</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Efficiency recovery possible</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-solar-green mb-2">Free</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Annual check for customers</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">How Soiling Affects Solar Panel Performance</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Photovoltaic modules convert sunlight into electricity through a chemical reaction in silicon cells. Any obstruction between the sun and the panel surface -- dust, salt, bird droppings, pollen, or industrial pollution -- reduces the photons reaching the cells, directly lowering power output. Unlike a dirty window, soiling on solar panels has an immediate and measurable financial impact.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Studies across Southeast Asia consistently show soiling losses of 15% to 25% after 3 to 6 months without cleaning. In extreme cases -- such as coastal Thailand during dry season when salt spray accumulates, or inland areas near construction sites where red dust coats panels -- losses can reach 30% to 40%. A 5 kilowatt-peak system producing 25 kWh daily when clean may drop to 17 kWh with heavily soiled panels, costing you 8 kWh of free electricity every single day.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Soiling also creates localised heating. When part of a panel is shaded by debris, that section stops generating power and becomes a resistive load, heating up the surrounding cells. This thermal imbalance can trigger bypass diodes and reduce string output. Over time, persistent hot spots accelerate Potential Induced Degradation (PID) -- a slow but permanent loss in panel performance caused by voltage stress and moisture ingress.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Thailand-Specific Soiling Challenges</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-100">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Red Dust and Fine Particles</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Dry season in Northeast and Central Thailand brings fine red dust from agricultural burning and unpaved roads. This iron-rich soil adheres strongly to glass surfaces and requires careful removal to avoid micro-scratches that permanently reduce light transmission.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-100">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Salt Spray (Coastal Areas)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Koh Chang, Pattaya, Phuket, and other coastal locations face salt crystallisation on panel surfaces. Salt is hygroscopic -- it attracts moisture -- and creates conductive pathways that can accelerate corrosion of aluminium frames and junction boxes if not regularly removed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-100">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Bird Droppings and Organic Debris</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Bird droppings contain uric acid that etches anti-reflective coatings over time. Fruit bats, common in Thai fruit-growing regions, leave large droppings that create significant shaded areas. Trees overhanging roofs drop leaves, flowers, and sap that stick to panels during humid weather.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-100">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2">Monsoon Deposits</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">While rain washes away loose dust, monsoon storms also deposit mud, leaves, and airborne pollutants. First rain after dry season often leaves a film of dissolved particulates that dries into a hazy layer, reducing transmission by 5% to 10% even when panels look clean.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Our Cleaning Process and What You Get</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        We use deionised water and soft-bristle brushes designed specifically for photovoltaic glass. Deionised water leaves no mineral residue, unlike tap water which contains calcium and magnesium that form spots when it evaporates. Our technicians work from secure ladders or scaffolding -- never walking directly on panels, which can crack cells and void warranties.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-solar-green text-xl">✓</span> <span class="text-gray-700"><strong>Visual inspection:</strong> Before cleaning, we photograph each panel and check for cracks, delamination, discolouration, or hot spots using a thermal camera.</span></li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-solar-green text-xl">✓</span> <span class="text-gray-700"><strong>Deionised water rinse:</strong> Low-pressure spray removes loose debris without forcing water into junction boxes or under frame seals.</span></li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-solar-green text-xl">✓</span> <span class="text-gray-700"><strong>Soft-brush agitation:</strong> Biodegradable cleaning solution breaks down stubborn deposits. Brushes have flagged tips that cannot scratch tempered glass.</span></li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-solar-green text-xl">✓</span> <span class="text-gray-700"><strong>Final rinse and dry:</strong> Squeegee removal of excess water prevents streaking. We clean in early morning or late afternoon to avoid thermal shock on hot panels.</span></li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-solar-green text-xl">✓</span> <span class="text-gray-700"><strong>Performance verification:</strong> We measure power output before and after cleaning, providing a report showing energy recovery in watts and percentage.</span></li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-solar-green text-xl">✓</span> <span class="text-gray-700"><strong>Maintenance recommendations:</strong> We advise on optimal cleaning frequency for your location -- typically every 3 months for coastal properties, every 6 months for inland areas.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Recommended Cleaning Schedule by Location</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg">
          <h3 class="font-bold mb-2">Coastal / Island</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Koh Chang, Phuket, Pattaya, Hua Hin: Every 2-3 months. Salt spray accumulates rapidly and requires frequent removal to prevent corrosion.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg">
          <h3 class="font-bold mb-2">Urban / Industrial</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Bangkok, Chiang Mai, industrial zones: Every 3-4 months. Air pollution and construction dust create persistent soiling layers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-50 p-5 rounded-lg">
          <h3 class="font-bold mb-2">Rural / Agricultural</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Northeast, Central plains: Every 4-6 months. Burning season (Feb-Apr) requires immediate post-season cleaning.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-50 to-blue-50 rounded-xl p-8 mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Related Resources</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <a href="/guides/solar-panel-maintenance" class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow border border-gray-100">
          <div class="font-bold">Maintenance Guide</div>
          <div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Learn to monitor and maintain your solar system between professional visits</div>
        </a>
        <a href="/guides/solar-system-sizing" class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow border border-gray-100">
          <div class="font-bold">System Sizing Calculator</div>
          <div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Account for soiling losses when sizing your solar array</div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="bg-solar-green text-white rounded-xl p-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">Restore Your System's Performance</h2>
      <p class="mb-6">Book professional solar panel cleaning at 300 THB per panel plus travel. Free annual inspection included for existing customers.</p>
      <a href="/#contact" class="inline-block bg-white text-solar-green font-semibold px-8 py-3 rounded-lg hover:bg-gray-100 transition-colors">
        Book Cleaning Service
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
